```java
import java.util.Stack;
class Solution {
    public String removeDuplicates(String s) {
        Stack<Character> st = new Stack<>();

        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (!st.isEmpty() && st.peek() == ch) {
                st.pop();
            } else {
                st.push(ch);
            }
        }
        StringBuilder ans = new StringBuilder();
        while (!st.isEmpty()) {
            ans.append(st.pop());
        }
        return ans.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```
```
Logic to Remember
st.peek() → checks the top character.
If the top equals the current character → st.pop().
Otherwise → st.push(ch).
Finally, convert the stack into a string.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)







// by just stringBuilder that avt like stack  

```java
class Solution {
    public String removeDuplicates(String s) {
        StringBuilder st = new StringBuilder();

        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            int n = st.length();

            if (n > 0 && st.charAt(n - 1) == ch) {
                st.deleteCharAt(n - 1);
            } else {
                st.append(ch);
            }
        }

        return st.toString();
    }
}
```

Logic to Remember
Traverse the string character by character.
If the current character equals the last character in the stack, remove the last character.
Otherwise, add the current character.
Return the remaining string.

Why does this work? Removing a duplicate can create a new adjacent pair, and the stack handles that automatically.

Complexity:
Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)

Interview tip: StringBuilder acts like a stack here. append() pushes a character, and deleteCharAt(n - 1) pops the top character.

